course: chemistry
lesson: 22
title: Milk, cheese and yoghurt
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Leave a glass of milk on a sunny windowsill and by evening it smells sour and has lumps in it. Bacteria have eaten the milk sugar and made acid, and the acid made the milk proteins clump.
explain: People learned to use this on purpose. Friendly bacteria turn milk into tangy yoghurt; bacteria and a substance called rennet split milk into solid curds and watery whey, and the curds become cheese, the curds and whey of Little Miss Muffet.
explain: Milk is also full of tiny fat droplets. Shake cream in a jar long enough and the droplets stick together into butter, leaving buttermilk behind; whisk it gently instead and the fat holds air bubbles as whipped cream.
explain: Heat milk and a skin forms on top, which traps steam until the milk suddenly rushes over the edge of the pan. And ice cream feels smooth because it is stirred while freezing, keeping its ice crystals tiny.
fact: Milk goes sour when bacteria eat the milk sugar and make acid, which makes the proteins clump; cold slows the bacteria, so milk keeps longer in the fridge.
fact: Cheese-makers add bacteria and rennet to milk, which separates into solid curds and watery whey; the curds become cheese.
fact: The blue-green veins in blue cheese are a safe mould added on purpose; other moulds on food can be harmful.
fact: Shaking cream for a long time breaks the coats on its fat droplets so they stick together into butter, leaving liquid buttermilk.
fact: A skin forms on hot milk as proteins and fat at the surface dry a little and join; stirring or covering the pan stops it.
fact: Milk boils over because its skin traps steam bubbles until they push the milk over; water has no skin, so it does not.
fact: Live yoghurt contains living bacteria that are harmless or helpful; a spoonful in warm milk can make more yoghurt.
fact: Lactose intolerance means the body cannot digest milk sugar well, because some people stop making the enzyme that breaks it down (an enzyme is a helper substance the body makes to break food apart).
fact: Lactose-free milk has the enzyme added, which breaks the milk sugar into simpler sugars that taste sweeter.
fact: Whisking cream traps air bubbles, held by fat droplets; whisking too long turns it lumpy and then into butter.
fact: Ice cream is stirred while freezing so the ice crystals stay tiny; melted and refrozen ice cream grows big crystals and feels crunchy.
exercise: Why does milk go sour when left out? | Bacteria eat its sugar and make acid | The sun turns the milk into lemon juice | Cold air freezes the proteins into lumps | The milk soaks up smells from the room
exercise: Why does milk go sour faster in summer? | Bacteria grow faster in the warmth | Summer air has much more lemon in it | The sun dries the milk out | Cows give sour milk in summer
exercise: What are curds and whey? | Solid lumps for cheese and the watery liquid left | Two kinds of butter, one hard and one soft and runny | Cream and skimmed milk | Two kinds of yoghurt
exercise: What do cheese-makers add to milk? | Bacteria and rennet | Salt and vinegar only | Sugar and baker's yeast | Flour and beaten eggs
exercise: Why is the mould in blue cheese OK? | It is a safe kind added on purpose | All moulds are perfectly safe to eat | The cheese kills the mould before you eat it | It is not mould at all, just blue dye
exercise: How does cream turn into butter? | Long shaking makes the fat droplets stick together | Heating boils all the water out of it until it sets | Adding salt turns it solid | Freezing makes it into butter
exercise: Where does the liquid come from when cream turns to butter? | It is the buttermilk left after the fat clumps | It is extra water added by all the hard shaking | It is melted butter | It leaks in from the jar lid
exercise: Why does hot milk get a skin? | Proteins and fat at the surface dry and join | Dust falls in from the kitchen air and forms a layer | The milk is turning into cheese | Air freezes on top of it
exercise: Why does milk boil over but water doesn't? | Milk's skin traps steam until it bursts over | Milk boils at a much, much lower temperature than water | Water is heavier than milk | Milk has gas squeezed into it
exercise: What does "live" yoghurt mean? | It contains living bacteria | It was made fresh only this morning | It moves by itself when you stir it | It has pieces of fresh fruit in it
exercise: How can you make yoghurt from yoghurt? | Add a spoonful of live yoghurt to warm milk | Freeze a pot of yoghurt overnight in the freezer | Boil the yoghurt for an hour | Mix it with lemon and salt
exercise: Why can't some people drink milk comfortably? | They don't make enough of the enzyme for milk sugar | They are allergic to anything that is coloured white | Milk is too cold for their stomachs | Their teeth react with the calcium
exercise: Why does lactose-free milk taste sweeter? | The milk sugar is broken into sweeter sugars | Extra spoonfuls of sugar are stirred in at the factory | The fat has been taken out | It is made from sweet almonds
exercise: What does whisking do to cream? | Traps air bubbles held by fat | Turns it into milk again | Melts the fat completely | Adds sugar from the whisk
exercise: Why does over-whipped cream go lumpy? | The fat clumps together towards butter | The tiny air bubbles inside it all freeze solid | Too much sugar comes out | The cream goes sour
exercise: Why does refrozen ice cream feel crunchy? | Big ice crystals grew as it refroze | Sugar turned into salt | Air turned into ice | The cream turned into butter
test: "milk's been on the windowsill all day, it's lumpy and stinks. what happened?" | Bacteria made acid that clumped it | It froze and thawed in the sun | Air turned it into cheese wax | Flies laid eggs in it
test: Max says blue cheese is fine with mould, so mouldy bread is fine too. What's true? | Only the chosen mould in blue cheese is safe | He's right, all mould is fine | Bread mould is even safer to eat than cheese mould | Both are poisonous every time
test: Grandma shakes cream in a jar for ten minutes. What will she end up with? | Butter and buttermilk | Whipped cream and air | Cheese and whey | Yoghurt and juice
test: Mum turns away from the hot milk for a minute and it rushes over the pan. Why so suddenly? | Its skin trapped steam until it burst | Milk boils twice as hot as water | The pan was too small | The milk was secretly fizzy
test: If you stirred a pan of hot milk the whole time, what would happen to the skin? | It would hardly form | It would grow thicker | It would turn into butter | It would sink to the bottom
test: A cartoon shows yoghurt bacteria as monsters attacking a tummy. What's true of live yoghurt bacteria? | They are harmless or helpful | They are dangerous germs that make you ill | They die the moment you open the pot | They turn into mould in your tummy
test: "my friend can't have milk, she says it's lactose. is she just fussy?" | No, her body can't digest milk sugar well | Yes, everyone can drink milk if they just try | Yes, lactose is just a flavour | No, she is allergic to cows themselves
test: Ava whisks cream for the cake and keeps going "to make it extra fluffy". What happens? | It goes lumpy and starts turning to butter | It gets fluffier for ever | It turns back into milk | It goes sour
test: The ice cream melted on the drive home and Dad refroze it. Why is it now crunchy? | Big ice crystals formed as it refroze | The sugar went off in the car | The car heat turned it to butter | Air got into the tub
test: "how do u even make cheese from milk???" Which is closest to the truth? | Add bacteria and rennet, then keep the curds | Boil it for hours and hours until it goes solid | Freeze it and grate it | Mix it with flour and bake it
test: If you put milk in the fridge instead of on the table, what would happen? | It would stay fresh longer | It would go sour faster | It would turn to cheese | It would boil over
exam: Identify the principal cause of the souring of milk left at room temperature. | Bacteria convert the milk sugar into acid | Light converts the milk into lemon juice | Oxygen converts the milk into butter | The milk absorbs acid from the atmosphere
exam: It is given that cream is agitated vigorously for a prolonged period. Select the outcome most consistent with the established facts. | The fat droplets coalesce into butter, leaving buttermilk | The cream is converted into cheese and whey | The cream becomes sour through the action of rennet | The cream evaporates entirely into the air
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises lactose intolerance? | The body digests milk sugar poorly, owing to insufficient enzyme | The body is allergic to all foods and drinks containing any water | The body rejects milk owing to its colour | The body produces excessive quantities of milk sugar
exam: Ascertain the reason for which milk, unlike water, is liable to boil over. | A surface skin retains steam until it forces the milk over the edge | Milk boils at a temperature far below that of water | Milk contains dissolved gas introduced at the dairy | Milk is lighter than water and rises spontaneously
